                <pb facs="0316" n="204" sameAs="frus:frus1919Russia/pg204"/>

                <note rend="inline" type="source">861.00/4728: Telegram</note>
                <head><hi rendition="#italic">The Minister in China</hi> (<hi rendition="#italic"
                        >Reinsch</hi>) <hi rendition="#italic">to the Acting Secretary of
                    State</hi></head>
                <opener><dateline rendition="#float-right"><placeName><hi rendition="#small-caps"
                                >Peking</hi></placeName>, <date
                            ana="#date_undated-inferred-from-document-content" calendar="gregorian"
                            notAfter="1919-06-21T12:24:00-05:00"
                            notBefore="1919-06-20T09:00:00+04:53"><hi rendition="#italic"
                                >undated</hi></date>.<lb/>[Received June 21, 1919, 12:24
                        p.m.]</dateline></opener>
                <p>From [Harris at] Omsk.</p>
                <p>“270, June 20, 9 a.m. General Gaida returned suddenly and unexpectedly to Omsk
                    yesterday. I had interview with him. Gaida is despondent, has aged in
                    appearance, and is discouraged as to future of military situation. Informed me
                    that in all probability he would retire and eventually go to France. He said
                    Kolchak was surrounded by a clique of Russian officers who were intriguing
                    against him. Gaida believes Bolsheviki will not be defeated by Siberian Army and
                    that overthrow of Bolshevism is not yet in sight. Bolsheviki are larger in
                    number and now are [better?] equipped with guns than Siberian forces.
                    Harris.”</p>
                <closer><signed><hi rendition="#small-caps">Reinsch</hi></signed></closer>
            </div>
